[size=5][b]PlayStation buttons instead of Xbox ones.[/b][/size]

Dungeons II shows Xbox prompts to every PC player, whatever you have plugged in. This swaps them for PlayStation ones. There is a Nintendo Switch version too, under optional files.

[img]PUT_YOUR_IMAGE_URL_HERE[/img]

I sat down with a DualSense and the game spent the whole evening telling me to press A. My controller does not have an A. This does not pass the significant other / kid check.

No new artwork. The PlayStation icons are already sitting in your install, fully drawn. The game just never picks them, so this puts them where the game is already looking.

[size=4][b]Installation[/b][/size]

[list=1]
[*]Download the one you want. [b]PlayStation[/b] is the main file, [b]Nintendo Switch[/b] is under optional files. They install exactly the same way.
[*]Unzip it. You get three files, all named [font=Courier New]Dungeons-Windows_P[/font]
[*]Drop all three into your game's [b]Paks[/b] folder
[*]Play
[/list]

[b]Steam[/b]
[font=Courier New]...\steamapps\common\Minecraft Dungeons II\Dungeons\Content\Paks[/font]

[b]Microsoft Store and Game Pass[/b]
In the Xbox app, find the game, then [i]Manage[/i] > [i]Files[/i] > [i]Browse[/i]. From the folder that opens, go into [font=Courier New]Dungeons\Content\Paks[/font]

Keep the three files together and do not rename them. They are one container split across three files, and the game ignores an incomplete set.

[size=4][b]Switching between versions[/b][/size]

Both downloads use the same three filenames, so they overwrite each other. Copy the other one over the top and you are done. You cannot run both at once.

[size=4][b]Uninstall[/b][/size]

Delete the three [font=Courier New]Dungeons-Windows_P[/font] files. Nothing else was touched. Verifying your files through Steam also removes them, which is worth knowing if the mod ever seems to vanish on its own.

[size=4][b]Notes[/b][/size]

[list]
[*]Every controller gets these prompts, so an Xbox pad will show them too
[*]The controller picture on the System screen is still an Xbox one, coming in a later version
[*]Cosmetic only, safe in multiplayer
[*]The Switch version has not been tested on real hardware. It is built the same way from the game's own Switch icons. Tell me how you get on.
[*]On Switch, buttons are mapped by what they do rather than where they sit, so confirm shows as A and back shows as B
[*]Built for 1.1.1.0
[/list]

[size=4][b]Why it happens[/b][/size]

The game registers every controller profile on PC and then asks for the one called [i]Generic[/i]. The Xbox profile is the one that claims that name, so everyone gets Xbox buttons. You cannot talk it into asking for a different one, so this changes what that profile draws instead.
